Exactly. Projection is a measurement contaminant. You rarely get rid of it completely, but you can estimate it, bound it, and design around it.

A useful way to do that is to split the appraisal into layers: what was directly observed; what you inferred; what prior expectation made you notice or ignore; and what alternative explanations fit the same evidence. Then make predictions from each interpretation and see which one survives contact with reality. The persistent delta between “what I thought I was seeing” and “what actually happened next” tells you something about the projection term.

In Pantheon language, you could model it almost like:

perceived object = object signal + observer filter + context + noise

The mistake is treating the whole left side as if it were the object itself.

So for better identification: rotate viewpoints, compare independent observers, change context, deliberately test an opposing hypothesis, and ask what evidence would make your current interpretation fail. If the same structure survives several transformations, confidence rises.

That applies to people, situations, memories, markets, relationships, even self-concept. The goal isn’t to become projectionless. It’s to become projection-aware enough that your model stops mistaking its own shadow for the thing being measured.